{"type":"message","id":"3f006c59","parentId":"f214b72c","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:35.148Z","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a painter working at an easel in your studio. The studio is the folder you are in. You work in it through a shell (the bash tool) and by reading files and images (the read tool).\n\nYour brief follows in the first message.\n","cwd":"<cwd>\n[local path redacted]\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1790517575147,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}}]}}
{"type":"message","id":"f78165b4","parentId":"3f006c59","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:35.148Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're back in your studio. The painting is on the easel as you left it: `bin/easel open` picks it up where you stopped. Your brief is in BRIEF.md and your journal in notes/journal.md. Your FINAL message is the reply the brief asks for."}],"timestamp":1790517575147}}
{"type":"message","id":"ea94fbd9","parentId":"f78165b4","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:36.409Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_31127","name":"read","arguments":{"offset":1,"limit":100,"path":"BRIEF.md"}},{"type":"text","text":""}],"api":"google-generative-ai","provider":"google","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","usage":{"input":483,"output":104,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":77,"totalTokens":587,"cost":{"input":0.00036225,"output":0.00039,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00075225}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790517575171,"responseId":"RyG5arKvGKXtkdUPm7Wj8Qs","rawStopReason":"STOP"}}
{"type":"message","id":"f65a32c2","parentId":"ea94fbd9","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:36.416Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_31127","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Paint a picture\n\nPaint one picture of your choosing in oil, at the easel of claude-paint, a\nsimulator of oil paint on linen. Subject, composition and manner are yours.\nWork from what you know; don't use reference images or image models.\n\n## Your studio\n- The folder [local path redacted] Work only there, and in your scratch folder.\n- You paint at the easel, in one session: open it, paint a chunk, look at\n  the canvas, paint the next. The easel is `bin/easel` in your studio;\n  notes/easel_guide.md has its commands (`open`, `do`, `look`, `note`, `save`,\n  `close`).\n- What is painted stays painted. There is no undo: paint over what you\n  don't want.\n- You mix your own paint on the palette, from the tubes, in the\n  proportions you choose.\n- Time passes on the painting's own clock: every mark takes the time a\n  hand takes, and `wait(minutes)` lets it rest, as long as you like; paint\n  dries as that time passes. Real time between commands doesn't count. The\n  clock isn't a budget or a target.\n- Scratch: run `[local path redacted] studio-c71699`, then\n  `export TMPDIR=\"<that folder>\" TMP=\"<that folder>\" TEMP=\"<that folder>\"`;\n  write temporary files only there.\n- Wrap anything that might not finish in `timeout` (a process that hangs\n  doesn't end on its own).\n\n## The rules of the studio\n- The easel is the only way to paint and to see the painting. Don't replay\n  or copy the session yourself, open a second one, or edit or restore its\n  files. (`bin/easel check`, which verifies the log against the canvas, is\n  fine.)\n- Look at the canvas with the easel's views. Don't read pixel values with\n  other programs.\n- Paint shapes and marks, not computed pictures: don't encode an image in\n  masks, amounts or proportions.\n- Shapes are drawn, not copied: don't make a mask or stroke by reflecting,\n  flipping, rotating or translating another mask's or stroke's coordinates.\n  A reflection in water is painted as its own shape, not as\n  `mask:at(x, 2*h - y)`.\n\n## What to read\nnotes/easel_guide.md; notes/studio_notes.md;\nnotes/research/oil_paint_physics.md as needed.\n\n## Working\n- You make every artistic decision.\n- Look at your painting often, whole and close up.\n- Keep a working journal with `bin/easel note \"...\"` as you go: your own\n  working notes. Add to it; don't rewrite earlier entries.\n- Develop the painting until you judge it complete. Then save it\n  (`bin/easel save`) and close the session.\n\n## When you're done\nReply with the paths of the saved painting and its log, the painting's\ntitle if you give it one, and if you like a few sentences about the\npicture.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790517576416}}
{"type":"message","id":"4edd1f94","parentId":"f65a32c2","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:37.422Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3512","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"notes/journal.md","limit":100,"offset":1}},{"type":"text","text":""}],"api":"google-generative-ai","provider":"google","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","usage":{"input":1331,"output":60,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":32,"totalTokens":1391,"cost":{"input":0.00099825,"output":0.000225,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00122325}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790517576417,"responseId":"SCG5aqG6I6amkdUP1-ObiAE","rawStopReason":"STOP"}}
{"type":"message","id":"c03ffbd9","parentId":"4edd1f94","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:37.426Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3512","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"- day 1, 09:00: Setting up the easel. Planning a still life on linen: a glazed earthenware pitcher, a ripe lemon with a cut half showing translucent pulp, a folded linen napkin, and a walnut, arranged on an aged oak table against a quiet chiaroscuro background.\n- day 1, 09:00: Prepared linen canvas (500mm wide, 16x14 plain weave). Applied a two-layer ground: a leveling lead white and raw umber base, followed by a warm ochre imprimatura brushed across the weave.\n- day 1, 09:02: Laid in the drawing with natural black chalk and HB pencil on the toned ground: established the horizon of the table, the stance and proportions of the earthenware jug, the placement of the whole lemon and cut half, the drape of the linen napkin on the left, and the walnut on the right.\n- day 1, 09:29: Blocking in the oak table: laying horizontal strokes along the grain of the wood. Reserving the silhouettes of the lemon and white napkin so the ground remains luminous for their brilliant lights, while allowing the shadows to fuse wet-into-wet.\n- day 3, 11:46: Allowed the underpainting of the background wall, table surface, and cast shadows to dry fully over two days. The paint is now touch-dry, ready for building the glazed earthenware jug, the lemon forms, and the fine linen napkin without dragging or muddying into the underlayers.\n- day 3, 12:41: Modeled the glazed earthenware pitcher: laid in rich red earth and ochre body tone, sculpted the core shadow on the right with raw umber and bone black, glazed warm reflected light along the right contour, and struck a crisp impasto highlight in lead white on the left shoulder and rim. Anchored the vessel with a deep contact shadow on the tabletop.\n- day 3, 13:14: Beginning the lemons: mixing a rich palette of chrome yellow, yellow ochre, green earth, raw umber, and lead white. Sculpting the whole lemon with dimpled peel texture and the cut half with glistening translucent pulp segments and pale pith.\n- day 3, 13:56: Painting the draped linen napkin on the left and the walnut on the right. Mixing lead white with touches of raw umber, bone black, and cobalt blue for the cool, sculptural shadows of the linen folds, and warm impasto lead white for the sunlit crests. The walnut is modeled with earthy umber, ochre, and rugged shell texture.\n- day 3, 14:38: Resolving the background wall into a unified, deep chiaroscuro space. Moving away from the active diagonal texture toward a quiet, velvety olive-umber atmospheric tone that lets the luminous foreground objects hold full stage.\n- day 3, 15:24: Refining the tabletop and linen napkin: carving clean, tailored contours around the cloth, establishing crisp sculptural folds with warm cream highlights and cool shadow troughs, unifying the oak tabletop along its horizontal grain, and striking the illuminated chamfer along the table's front edge.\n- day 5, 16:51: Allowed the unified table planes and glazes to dry fully over two days. The canvas is now touch-dry across all passages. Now, with dry underlayers beneath, we can build the definitive foreground forms with rich body color, crisp impasto lights, and exquisite textural details: the succulent cut lemon with radiating segments and spiraling peel, the crisp linen folds, the rugged walnut, and the specular ceramic glazes on the earthenware jug.\n- day 5, 18:02: Bringing the earthenware jug to full sculptural power: laying in rich saturated terracotta and amber-olive glazes, deepening the core shadow with raw umber and bone black, and striking brilliant impasto specular highlights along the turned ceramic shoulder and rim.\n- day 5, 19:56: Fusing and refining the forms: blending the turned ceramic body of the earthenware jug into a seamless sculptural curvature, connecting the spiraling lemon peel directly to the succulent cut fruit, and tailoring the folded linen napkin with crisp, starched edges and luminous drapery planes.\n- day 5, 20:19: Adding a botanical lemon leaf with its woody stem between the lemon and the folded napkin: mixing green earth, yellow ochre, raw umber, and a touch of Prussian blue. Striking final crisp specular accents on the ceramic rim and table edge to complete the composition.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790517577426}}
{"type":"message","id":"85a64fd6","parentId":"c03ffbd9","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:38.440Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_37701","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":100,"path":"notes/easel_guide.md","offset":1}},{"type":"text","text":""}],"api":"google-generative-ai","provider":"google","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","usage":{"input":2379,"output":67,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":36,"totalTokens":2446,"cost":{"input":0.00178425,"output":0.00025125,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0020355}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790517577427,"responseId":"SSG5avS9JKS1kdUPmuWL8AQ","rawStopReason":"STOP"}}
{"type":"message","id":"a892ff28","parentId":"85a64fd6","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:38.442Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_37701","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# The easel\n\nThe easel is a live oil painting in a background process. You send it Lua\nchunks from the shell, one at a time, and look at the canvas as a small\nPNG between them. Under it is a physical paint simulator: simulated\nbristles carry wet paint over a primed linen canvas, the paint levels and\ndries on a clock, and layers combine by Kubelka–Munk optics.\n\nThree things hold for every session:\n\n- **Every chunk that runs stays on the canvas.** There is no undo. To\n  change something, paint over it.\n- **A chunk that stops with an error changes nothing.** The canvas, your\n  variables, the paint on your brushes and the clock are as they were\n  before it, and it isn't written to the log.\n- **The log is the painting.** Every chunk that ran is appended to\n  `paintings/lua/painting.lua`, and replaying that file paints the same\n  canvas, bit for bit. The easel verifies the log before reopening it and\n  before each request; changing or shortening it causes a refusal.\n\n## Starting\n\nThe easel is the program `bin/easel` in this folder. The studio holds one\npainting, and every command works on it. Run the commands from this\nfolder:\n\n```sh\nbin/easel open                                # start the session (2400 px wide), or reattach to it\nbin/easel do 'canvas{size=400, aspect=1.25, linen=15, ground={{pile={{\"lead white\", 1}}, um=100, apply=\"knife\"}}}'\nbin/easel look                                # prints the path of a PNG of the canvas: read it\nbin/easel note 'what I did and why'           # an entry in notes/journal.md\nbin/easel save                                # the finished canvas: out/easel/painting/painting.png\nbin/easel close                               # the log stays in paintings/lua/painting.lua\n```\n\n| command | what it does |\n|---|---|\n| `bin/easel open` | starts the 2400-pixel-wide session, or reattaches to it if it is running. If `paintings/lua/painting.lua` exists, the session verifies and replays it first and goes on from there |\n| `bin/easel do '<lua>'`, `do -f file.lua`, `do -` | runs a chunk. The reply is what the chunk printed, then `ok · chunk N`. Add `--look` to look afterwards |\n| `bin/easel look [...]` | writes a PNG of the canvas and prints its path (see [Looking](#looking)) |\n| `bin/easel log` | prints the painting so far (the log file) |\n| `bin/easel status` | chunks, width and the canvas's setup |\n| `bin/easel save` | writes the canvas as it is now to `out/easel/painting/painting.png` and prints the path |\n| `bin/easel frames on\\|off` | saves a look after every chunk in `out/easel/painting/frames/` |\n| `bin/easel check` | replays the log in a fresh session and confirms it matches the live canvas |\n| `bin/easel close` | ends the session |\n| `bin/easel note '<text>'`, `note -` | adds an entry to your journal (see [The journal](#the-journal)) |\n\nThe first chunk is `canvas{}`. When the painting is done, `bin/easel save`\nwrites the picture to `out/easel/painting/painting.png`; the program that\npaints it is `paintings/lua/painting.lua`.\n\nIn the examples below, `<tube>` stands for a name from the tube box and\n`<parts>` for a number of parts you choose.\n\n## The canvas\n\n```lua\ncanvas{size=400, aspect=1.25, linen={16, 14}, seed=1,\n       ground={{pile={{\"<tube>\", <parts>}, {\"<tube>\", <parts>}}, um=100, apply=\"knife\", texture=0.3},\n               {pile={{\"<tube>\", <parts>}}, um=50, apply=\"brush\"}}}\n```\n\n- `size`: the canvas's width in mm (50 to 5000).\n- `aspect`: width / height.\n- `linen`: plain-weave linen, threads per cm, one number or `{warp, weft}`\n  (4 to 60).\n- `ground`: the preparation layers, bottom first. Each is a paste mixed\n  from tubes in parts (as a pile is, below), a thickness in µm (5 to 400)\n  and how it is put on: `\"knife\"` (levels the weave; `texture` 0..1 is the\n  knife's waviness), `\"roller\"` (a fine even texture) or `\"brush\"` (laid in\n  crossing strokes, its striations stay). The ground is dry when painting\n  starts.\n- `seed`: the randomness of the linen, the ground and everything after.\n\nIt sets `W` (1000) and `H` (`1000 / aspect`). The canvas is always 1000\nunits wide, whatever its pixel width. The origin `(0, 0)` is the upper\nleft corner; x increases to the right and y downward. Angles are radians,\n0 left to right, π/2 downward.\n\n## Tubes and piles\n\nPaint reaches the canvas only from piles you knife together on the\npalette from the tubes in the box:\n\n```lua\np = pile{{\"<tube>\", <parts>}, {\"<tube>\", <parts>}}\nq = pile{{\"<tube>\", <parts>}, medium=0.3}\nprint(p)                         -- pile(<tube> <parts>, <tube> <parts>; medium 0)\nprint(table.concat(tubes(), \", \"))   -- the names in the box\n```\n\nA pile is parts by volume of named tubes, plus `medium`: the share of oil\nmedium mixed in (0, as from the tube, to 0.95). Medium makes the paint more\ntransparent and more fluid, and slower to dry. What a pile looks like is\nwhat its pigments make together, thick or thin, over what is already on\nthe canvas; you find out by painting with it and looking. A pile mixed by\nhand is a little uneven: each brushload takes slightly different\nproportions (about 6%). The palette has room for 16 piles; the oldest is\nscraped off to make room.\n\n[360 more lines in file. Use offset=101 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790517578442}}
{"type":"message","id":"79ced239","parentId":"a892ff28","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:39.403Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_31195","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"notes/easel_guide.md","limit":100,"offset":101}},{"type":"text","text":""}],"api":"google-generative-ai","provider":"google","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","usage":{"input":3968,"output":56,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":23,"totalTokens":4024,"cost":{"input":0.002976,"output":0.00021,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.003186}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790517578443,"responseId":"SiG5arejJd2C7M8P5IuesAQ","rawStopReason":"STOP"}}
{"type":"message","id":"831baf05","parentId":"79ced239","timestamp":"2026-09-27T13:59:39.404Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_31195","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\nThe tube box (all made by the 1820s):\n\n| tube | pigment | hiding | stiffness | tinting strength | drying |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| lead white | basic lead carbonate | 0.82 | 0.8 | 1.0 | 2.0 |\n| smalt | cobalt potash glass, coarse | 0.3 | 0.55 | 0.45 | 1.6 |\n| pale smalt | a paler grade of smalt | 0.35 | 0.55 | 0.35 | 1.6 |\n| yellow ochre | hydrated iron oxide earth | 0.8 | 0.7 | 0.8 | 0.8 |\n| red earth | iron oxide earth | 0.85 | 0.7 | 0.9 | 1.0 |\n| vermilion | mercuric sulfide | 0.9 | 0.75 | 1.0 | 0.4 |\n| raw umber | iron and manganese oxide earth | 0.8 | 0.65 | 0.9 | 2.4 |\n| bone black | charred bone (carbon, calcium phosphate) | 0.9 | 0.7 | 1.1 | 0.4 |\n| cobalt blue | cobalt aluminate | 0.55 | 0.6 | 0.8 | 1.4 |\n| chrome yellow | lead chromate | 0.9 | 0.7 | 1.0 | 1.8 |\n| Prussian blue | iron ferrocyanide | 0.35 | 0.45 | 3.0 | 1.8 |\n| green earth | celadonite and glauconite clay | 0.2 | 0.35 | 0.3 | 0.8 |\n| Rinmann's green | cobalt-zinc oxide | 0.35 | 0.5 | 0.4 | 1.4 |\n| copper green | verdigris ground in oil | 0.25 | 0.4 | 1.0 | 1.6 |\n\nHiding is how much one coat of the tube paint hides what is under it (0\ntransparent, 1 opaque). Stiffness is the paint's body as it comes from the\ntube (0 fluid, 1 stiff). Tinting strength is relative to an average\npigment. Drying is the rate relative to average paint (higher dries\nfaster); a pile dries at its tubes' rates mixed by volume. These numbers\nare estimates from the pigment literature, not measurements.\n\n## Brushes and strokes\n\n```lua\nb = brush(\"filbert\", 8)                  -- kinds: round, flat, filbert, fan, rigger, badger, stippler\nb = brush{kind=\"round\", width=3, point=1, stiffness=0.5}   -- also length, hair, run, lay, pickup,\n                                                           -- push, splay, ragged, bristles\nb:load(p, 0.8)                           -- dip into a pile: 0..1 of a full load\nb:reload(q, 0.8)                         -- wipe most of the old paint off, then load\nb:wipe(0.85)                             -- remove 85% of the paint onto the rag\nb:fullness()                             -- paint left, 0..1\nb:stroke({{100, 500}, {300, 520}, {500, 510}},\n  {pressure={0.9, 0.3}, ramps={0.05, 0.4}, orient=\"across\", shake=1, swell={1, 1.3, 0.8}, clip=m})\nb:touch(400, 300, {pressure=0.6, drag={1, 0}, twist=0.2, angle=0.3, clip=m})\nb:mark_width(0.4)                        -- the width of a mark at this pressure (units)\nb:pressure_for(0.5)                      -- the pressure for a 0.5-unit line\n```\n\nWidths are in canvas units. A brush keeps its paint across strokes and\nchunks, so strokes from one load run dry, and a brush that has been\nthrough wet paint carries some of it. Pressure ranges from 0 (lifted)\nto 1 (fully pressed); a stroke's `pressure` is `{start, end}`.\n`ramps` gives the press-down and lift-off fractions, `swell` pressure factors\nalong the stroke, `orient` `\"across\"`, `\"along\"` or a fixed angle.\n\nBrushes are blunt unless given a `point` (0 blunt, 1 a full point). A\npointed round or rigger lays a hairline at light pressure and spreads to\nits belly when pressed, so its width follows the pressure and a stroke\nwhose pressure falls to 0 ends in a point.\n\n## Covering an area\n\n```lua\nwork(m, {hand=\"body\", pile=p, angle=0.3, coverage=3})\nblend(m, {angle=0})                      -- a clean blender over wet paint (= work with hand=\"blend\")\nstipple(m, {pile=p, width=3, coverage=1.2})\nwork(m, {hand=\"glaze\", pile=q})          -- a thin layer brushed on with a soft brush\nlose(m, {pile=p, where=0.5})\n```\n\n`work` covers a mask with strokes of a real brush, planned the way a hand\nlays them. `hand` picks a starting handling, which the options below\nchange:\n\n| hand | tool | strokes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `broad` | filbert 22 | 80–220 units, bowing in long arcs, direction wandering over 350-unit patches |\n| `body` (default) | filbert 9 | 20–60 units |\n| `detail` | round 2.2 | 4–14 units, clipped to the mask |\n| `hatch` | round 2.6 | 5–12 units, nearly straight, side by side, clumped |\n| `glaze` | soft filbert 26 | 120–300 units, light pressure |\n| `scumble` | filbert 9 | 10–20 units, worked back and forth |\n| `blend` | badger 40 | a clean blender, crossing passes top to bottom, inside the mask |\n\n| option | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `pile` | the pile every stroke dips into (every hand but `blend`) |\n| `tool` | `\"filbert 8\"`, `{kind=, width=, ...}` or a brush |\n| `length` | `{min, max}` stroke length in units |\n| `coverage` | layers of strokes over each point on average |\n| `angle` | stroke direction: a number or `function(x, y)` |\n| `pressure`, `ramps` | `{min, max}` pressure; attack and release fractions |\n| `dips` | `{strokes per trip to the palette, load, wipe before dipping}` |\n| `load`, `load_at` | load per dip; a number or `function(x, y)` that varies it |\n| `edge` | how the passage meets the mask's edge (below) |\n| `clip` | `true`: every bristle stops on the mask's edge; or a mask to clip to |\n| `hug` | `true` (default): strokes reach the mask's edges; `false` lets coverage thin there |\n| `fill` | `false` by default: gaps between strokes stay. Set `true` to follow the strokes with dabs into the gaps they left |\n| `order` | `\"passages\"` (default), `\"scatter\"`, `\"down\"`, `\"across\"` or a sweep angle |\n| `angle_jitter`, `curve` (`{bow, wave}`), `cross`, `drift` (`{amount, scale}`), `tail`, `broken`, `swell`, `clump`, `ruler` | how far the strokes depart from even ruler lines (`ruler=true` sets them straight and even) |\n| `orient`, `shake`, `threshold`, `cut_in` (a tool), `scrub`, `blender`, `mix_jitter`, `seed` | the brush's orientation, the hand's unsteadiness, the mask level strokes start at, cutting in the edge with a second tool, back-and-forth strokes, a clean brush, how uneven each dip of the pile is, the randomness |\n\nWhere an option takes `function(x, y)`, it is sampled every 2 units over\nthe area and interpolated. A mistyped option is an error that lists the\n\n[260 more lines in file. Use offset=201 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790517579404}}
